<!-- ════════════════ 03 · MENTAL MODEL ════════════════ -->
  <section data-label="Mental model" class="slide light cat-themed" style="--c: #4f46e5;">
    <div class="glow soft"></div>
    <div class="frame">
      <div class="cat-head">
        <span class="cat-icon">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 5a3 3 0 1 0-5.997.142 4 4 0 0 0-2.526 5.77 4 4 0 0 0 .556 6.588A4 4 0 1 0 12 18Z"/><path d="M12 5a3 3 0 1 1 5.997.142 4 4 0 0 1 2.526 5.77 4 4 0 0 1-.556 6.588A4 4 0 1 1 12 18Z"/></svg>
        </span>
        <div class="cat-text">
          <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">03</span>Mental model</p>
          <h2 class="h2">Why less context often <span class="accent">works better</span>.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="frame-body">
      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.35fr 0.8fr; gap: 36px; margin-top: 20px; align-items: stretch;">

        <!-- LEFT: context rot chart -->
        <div class="card">
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px;">
            <h3 style="margin: 0;">Context rot</h3>
            <span style="font-size: 18px; color: #64748b;">more context, less reliable answer</span>
          </div>

          <svg viewBox="0 0 760 300" width="100%" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" style="height: auto; display: block;" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: the same question is answered more reliably with a focused prompt of about 300 tokens than with the full context of about 113K tokens">
            <!-- baseline -->
            <line x1="60" y1="250" x2="740" y2="250" stroke="#cbd5e1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <!-- y axis label -->
            <text x="32" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#94a3b8" transform="rotate(-90 32 140)">answer reliability</text>

            <!-- Focused prompt -->
            <rect x="150" y="46" width="180" height="204" rx="6" fill="#4f46e5"/>
            <text x="240" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="600" fill="#4f46e5">high</text>
            <text x="240" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="600" fill="#334155">Focused prompt</text>
            <text x="240" y="296" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" fill="#64748b">~300 tokens</text>

            <!-- Volledige context -->
            <rect x="450" y="168" width="180" height="82" rx="6" fill="#d97706"/>
            <text x="540" y="156" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="600" fill="#d97706">much lower</text>
            <text x="540" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="600" fill="#334155">Full context</text>
            <text x="540" y="296" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" fill="#64748b">~113K tokens</text>
          </svg>

          <p style="font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.4; color: #64748b; margin: 4px 0 0;">Chroma (2025) tested 18 models including GPT-4.1, Claude 4, Gemini 2.5 and Qwen3. The same question is answered far more reliably with a focused prompt (~300 tokens) than the full context (~113K). Even simple tasks degrade as input grows.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- RIGHT: the two mechanisms -->
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          <div class="card" style="flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;">
            <h3>Attention budget</h3>
            <p>A model spreads its attention across all tokens together. The more you send, the thinner that attention gets per token. Compare it to someone who has to read too much at once.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 14px; color: #94a3b8; margin: 8px 0 0;">after Anthropic, &ldquo;Effective context engineering&rdquo; (2025)</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card" style="flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;">
            <h3>Lost in the middle</h3>
            <p>Information in the middle of a long prompt is missed most often. Put what matters at the top or the bottom.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 14px; color: #94a3b8; margin: 8px 0 0;">after Liu et al. (2023), &ldquo;Lost in the Middle&rdquo;</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        The rule of thumb for the whole course: find the smallest set of tokens with the most relevant information. <strong>Send signal, leave out noise</strong>.
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    {% set slideNumber = "03" %}
    {% set slideTotal = "57" %}
    {% include "../../shared/chrome.njk" %}
  </section>
